#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Which KVM test host has room for a job right now.

Usage: pick-host [OPTIONS]

The four amd64 hosts (testvm/hosts.json) are not interchangeable and not
equally busy: the big ones queue for hours while the small ones idle, and
nothing tells a session where to put its next run.  pick-host asks every
candidate host, over one ssh each, in parallel, what room it has
(`cmp-run.sh --free`, which writes nothing there), what it could ever hold
(`--ceiling`), how loaded it is, which guests it has, and which HoltTest
inputs; then it prints the host to use.  Nothing is started: the answer goes
in your own command.

    cd ~/git/backotter
    H=$(testvm/pick-host --reserve 8 5 --guest debian13 --needs debs) &&
      ssh "$H" 'cd ~/backotter-work && ...'

The figures are a moment old, and only a `cmp-run.sh --reserve` of its own
holds any room: another session may take it first.  pick-host is advice, not
a lock.

Candidates are the table's default, enabled hosts of the guests'
architecture (hosts.py guest_arch: a guest named *-arm64 is arm64, every
other amd64, and a run of amd64 guests never considers Bigfoot), unless
--hosts names rows itself, when a non-default row is taken as given too.  A
reserved row (enabled false) is no host to name, and the architecture is
worked out from --guest whatever --hosts says: a named host of the other
architecture is dropped with its reason, not probed.  A candidate is
dropped, with a reason, when it

  - doesn't answer (ssh failed, timed out, or the probe didn't finish);
  - is of another architecture than the guests, or its `uname -m` disagrees
    with its row;
  - can hold no job at all: its `cmp-run.sh --ceiling` failed, and the
    reason is what it said (no tmpfs at /mnt/bko-ram, say, which is not the
    same as having no room right now);
  - lacks one of --needs (zfs, rescuer, rescuer-uefi, debs: the caps
    t2lib/facts_host.py records, and the checks ht1-queue.sh makes);
  - lacks one of --guest (no ~/backotter-work/vms/GUEST/*.qcow2: a small
    host's cache holds the published guests only);
  - has a ceiling under --reserve, so the job can never fit there, however
    long one waits (cmp-run.sh refuses such a --reserve at once).

The rest are ranked: the hosts where --reserve fits now come first, so a job
that can start starts; then by capacity (how many times over the reserve
fits, min(room disk / D, room memory / M)), then by the lowest load per core.
"Fits now" and the capacity go by the room the job would be offered after the
host's cmp-run.sh line (--free's "after DISK MEM": the waiters it may not
pass come off first, so a host whose line would hold the job back doesn't
fit now, however much free room it has).  A host whose cmp-run.sh --free is
older than that line is judged conservatively: it fits now only when its
free room holds the reserve and nobody is in line there (that cmp-run.sh
serves its line strictly in order), and its note says to sync testvm/ there.  A host with the fleet or store-mirror role (Chopin) is
ranked as if its load were LOAD_PENALTY higher: it holds the fleet, talks to
Bigfoot and the store, every session reaches for it by habit, and its cooler
is the weakest of the four.  A host that answers but whose cmp-run.sh is too
old to know --free (the probe looks for the option in the script before it
asks, since an old one would take --free for a queue TAG and make the
ledger's files) has no free room to rank by: it keeps its place as a
candidate, last, with a note to sync testvm/ there.

Exit 0 when a host is printed.  That is the best candidate, which is not
always one the job fits in right now: when nothing fits, the roomiest host
that could ever hold it is printed anyway, and the job waits its turn in that
host's cmp-run.sh line (--json says which, in fits_now).  Exit 1 when every
candidate was dropped, or there was no candidate at all (each reason on
stderr), 2 on bad usage.

Options:
  --reserve D M   the job's cmp-run.sh --reserve figures, GiB of RAM disk and
                  of memory (default none: hosts are ranked by the room they
                  have, and only a host with no room at all counts as full)
  --guest G       a guest the job boots; repeatable, comma-separated.  Its
                  image must be on the host, and its name sets the
                  architecture of the candidates
  --needs N       zfs, rescuer, rescuer-uefi or debs; repeatable,
                  comma-separated
  --class C       only hosts of class C (big, small)
  --hosts H,H     these hosts instead of the table's default ones
  --exclude H,H   not these hosts
  --count N       the N best hosts, one a line, best first
  --all           every candidate that was not dropped, best first
  --json          the whole probe, the ranking and every dropped host's
                  reasons, as JSON (on stdout, whatever the exit status, the
                  same keys even when there is no candidate or the table is
                  bad: "error" then says why.  Only argparse's own usage
                  errors, which come before --json is known, are plain text)
  --field F       what to print: ssh (the row's USER@HOST, the default) or
                  name (the table's short name, for --hosts of other tools)
  --timeout S     seconds for one host's whole probe (default 20): the budget
                  covers both the row's ssh destination and its ip fallback
                  together, and a destination that runs it out ends that
                  host's probe

Needs python3 3.9 or newer, stdlib only; it runs under `python3 -I -B`.  The
probe and the ranking are separate (probe_hosts() and rank(), which takes the
parsed probes as plain dicts), so tests check the ranking without a host:
testvm/test2/tests/test_pick_host.py.
"""

import argparse
import concurrent.futures
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
import time

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
# A host with one of these roles is the busy, shared one (Chopin: the fleet, the
# store mirror and the Bigfoot link), so its load counts as LOAD_PENALTY higher.
SHARED_ROLES = ("fleet", "store-mirror")
LOAD_PENALTY = 4.0
NEEDS = {"zfs": "zfs", "rescuer": "rescuer", "rescuer-uefi": "rescuer-uefi", "debs": "debs"}
FIELDS = ("ssh", "name")
# The reserve the capacity score uses when there is none to go by: a host's score is
# then the smaller of its free GiB, so the roomiest host wins.
SCORE_FLOOR = 1.0


def _host_table():
    """testvm/test2/t2lib/hosts.py, the loader of the host table testvm/hosts.json, by its
    path (as drive.py, vm-update and bs2 load it: nothing here imports t2lib)."""
    p = os.path.join(HERE, "test2", "t2lib", "hosts.py")
    if not os.path.isfile(p):
        raise ImportError(f"pick-host: no host table loader {p} (testvm/hosts.json's)")
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("pick_hosts_table", p)
    m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(m)
    return m


# The probe: one sh per host, over ssh, reading only.  It prints "KEY VALUE" lines and
# "end" last, so a probe cut short (a dropped connection) is told from a host that
# answered.  cmp-run.sh --free and --ceiling write nothing at all, not even the ledger's
# files; everything else here is a read of /proc, /bkoscratch or vms/.
PROBE_SH = r'''
set -u
W=$HOME/backotter-work
L=/bkoscratch/holttest1d
echo "host $(hostname -s)"
echo "machine $(uname -m)"
if [ -x "$W/testvm/compare/cmp-run.sh" ]; then
    # Both calls report their exit status, and a failing one the line it died
    # on: cmp-run.sh exits 1 with "no tmpfs at /mnt/bko-ram" before it reads
    # either option, and a host that can hold no job at all must be told from
    # one that is merely out of room.  stderr is folded into stdout because
    # nothing here may write a file (not even /dev/null's neighbours), so the
    # figures are picked out by shape, from whichever line has them: a login
    # shell that writes to stderr of its own (the ssh client forwarding LC_*
    # to a host without the locale, say) must not be read as the answer, nor
    # make pick-host drop a host that is in fact fine.  A failing call's
    # reason is its last line, for the same reason: cmp-run.sh says it last.
    out=$(cd "$W" && testvm/compare/cmp-run.sh --ceiling 2>&1); rc=$?
    echo "ceiling_rc $rc"
    if [ "$rc" = 0 ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$out" | while read -r a b; do
            case $a in [0-9]*) echo "ceiling $a $b" ;; esac
        done
    else
        echo "ceiling_err $(printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | tail -n 1)"
    fi
    # An older cmp-run.sh has no --free at all: it would take "--free" for a
    # queue TAG and make the ledger's files before dying, so it is asked only
    # when the script itself knows the option.  Then no "free" line, and
    # pick-host says to sync testvm/ there.
    if grep -q -- --free "$W/testvm/compare/cmp-run.sh"; then
        out=$(cd "$W" && testvm/compare/cmp-run.sh --free 2>&1); rc=$?
        echo "free_rc $rc"
        if [ "$rc" = 0 ]; then
            printf '%s\n' "$out" | while read -r a b; do
                case $a in
                waiting) echo "waiting $b" ;;
                after) echo "line_room $b" ;;
                [0-9]*) echo "free $a $b" ;;
                esac
            done
        else
            echo "free_err $(printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | tail -n 1)"
        fi
    fi
else
    echo "no-cmp-run $W/testvm/compare/cmp-run.sh"
fi
echo "nproc $(nproc 2>/dev/null || echo 1)"
echo "load $(cut -d' ' -f1 /proc/loadavg 2>/dev/null)"
echo "memavail_kib $(awk '$1 == "MemAvailable:" { print $2 }' /proc/meminfo 2>/dev/null)"
# zpool where root's PATH has it, as ht1-queue.sh and facts_host.py look for it (the
# module alone is not enough: vm-mount needs the binary).
for p in /usr/sbin/zpool /sbin/zpool /usr/local/sbin/zpool /usr/bin/zpool /bin/zpool; do
    [ -f "$p" ] && [ -x "$p" ] && { echo "cap zfs"; break; }
done
[ -f "$L/rescuer/rescuer.qcow2" ] && echo "cap rescuer"
[ -f "$L/rescuer/rescuer-uefi.qcow2" ] && echo "cap rescuer-uefi"
if ls "$L"/bin/f2fs-tools_*.deb >/dev/null 2>&1 && ls "$L"/bin/jfsutils_*.deb >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "cap debs"
fi
# The guests whose image is here (vms/GUEST/*.qcow2, a link into the LRU cache on a
# small host, followed), as the drivers take it.
for g; do
    for q in "$W/vms/$g/"*.qcow2; do [ -f "$q" ] && { echo "vm $g"; break; }; done
done
echo end
'''


def parse_probe(text):
    """The probe's output -> its facts, whatever is missing left out:
        {"host", "machine", "free": (D, M), "ceiling": (D, M), "waiting": N,
         "line_room": (D, M), "nproc": N,
         "load": F, "memavail_gib": F, "caps": [...], "vms": [...], "ok": bool,
         "ceiling_rc": N, "ceiling_err": "...", "free_rc": N, "free_err": "..."}
    The _rc of each cmp-run.sh call and, when it failed, the line it died on: a host
    whose --ceiling failed can hold no job at all (no tmpfs at /mnt/bko-ram, say), which
    is not the same as having no room.  An older probe sends neither, and then the ceiling
    and the free room are simply missing, as before; a cmp-run.sh --free older than its
    "after" line sends no line_room (the room a newcomer is offered after the line).
    ok is False unless the probe finished (its "end" line).  A line that doesn't parse is
    ignored: an older probe's extra output never makes a host look capable."""
    out = {"caps": [], "vms": [], "ok": False}
    for line in str(text or "").splitlines():
        w = line.split()
        if not w:
            continue
        k, rest = w[0], w[1:]
        try:
            if k == "end":
                out["ok"] = True
            elif k in ("host", "machine") and len(rest) == 1:
                out[k] = rest[0]
            elif k in ("free", "ceiling", "line_room") and len(rest) == 2:
                out[k] = (float(rest[0]), float(rest[1]))
            elif k in ("waiting", "nproc", "ceiling_rc", "free_rc") and len(rest) == 1:
                out[k] = int(rest[0])
            elif k in ("ceiling_err", "free_err") and rest:
                out[k] = " ".join(rest)[:160]
            elif k == "load" and len(rest) == 1:
                out["load"] = float(rest[0])
            elif k == "memavail_kib" and len(rest) == 1:
                out["memavail_gib"] = round(int(rest[0]) / 1048576.0, 1)
            elif k == "cap" and len(rest) == 1 and rest[0] in NEEDS:
                out["caps"].append(rest[0])
            elif k == "vm" and len(rest) == 1:
                out["vms"].append(rest[0])
            elif k == "no-cmp-run" and rest:
                out["no_cmp_run"] = rest[0]
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return out


def ssh_argv(table, host, timeout):
    """The ssh arguments that reach HOST: its row's ssh destination, or, when the row has
    an ip, that too, so a host whose mDNS name fails is still probed (drive.py's
    fallback, decided there by what ssh says; here both are simply tried in turn)."""
    base = ["ssh", "-T", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", f"ConnectTimeout={min(6, timeout)}",
            "-o", "ControlMaster=no", "-o", "ControlPath=none"]
    dests = [[table.ssh(host)]]
    alt = table.ssh_fallback(host)
    if alt:
        dests.append(alt)
    return base, dests


def probe_one(table, host, guests, timeout):
    """One host's probe over ssh, read only: its facts (parse_probe), with "error" when it
    didn't answer.  Never raises.

    TIMEOUT is the whole budget for this host, both its destinations together, so that the
    ip fallback can't double what --timeout promised: a destination gets what is left of
    it, and a destination that ran the budget out ends the probe (waiting as long again on
    the other name would only hold up the hosts that did answer)."""
    base, dests = ssh_argv(table, host, timeout)
    cmd = "sh -s -- " + " ".join(shlex.quote(g) for g in guests)
    last = "no answer"
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout
    for dest in dests:
        left = deadline - time.monotonic()
        if left <= 0:
            break
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(base + dest + [cmd], input=PROBE_SH, capture_output=True,
                               text=True, timeout=left)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            last = f"timed out after {timeout}s"
            break
        except OSError as e:
            last = f"ssh: {e}"
            continue
        got = parse_probe(r.stdout)
        if got["ok"]:
            return got
        err = (r.stderr or "").strip().splitlines()
        last = (err[-1][:120] if err else f"the probe did not finish (exit {r.returncode})")
    return {"ok": False, "caps": [], "vms": [], "error": last}


def probe_hosts(table, hosts, guests, timeout=20):
    """Every host of HOSTS probed at once, one ssh each: {host: facts}."""
    if not hosts:
        return {}
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=len(hosts)) as pool:
        futures = {h: pool.submit(probe_one, table, h, guests, timeout) for h in hosts}
        return {h: f.result() for h, f in futures.items()}


def num(x):
    """A GiB figure as cmp-run.sh writes it: 13.0 -> "13", 9.5 -> "9.5"."""
    return f"{float(x):g}"


def fits(room, reserve):
    """Whether a --reserve (D, M) fits in ROOM (D, M), as cmp-run.sh's fits() does."""
    return room is not None and room[0] >= reserve[0] and room[1] >= reserve[1]


def score_of(free, reserve):
    """How many times over RESERVE fits in FREE: min(free D / D, free M / M), the capacity
    to rank roomy hosts by.  A reserve figure of 0 doesn't bind (nothing of that is asked
    for), and no free room at all scores 0."""
    if free is None:
        return 0.0
    parts = [free[i] / reserve[i] for i in (0, 1) if reserve[i] > 0]
    return min(parts) if parts else 0.0


def rank(probes, reserve=None, guests=(), needs=(), rows=None):
    """Rank the probed hosts: (ranked, dropped).

    PROBES is {host: parse_probe()'s facts}; ROWS {host: its table row} gives each host's
    roles and arch (left out: no role counts, and the architecture is not checked).
    RESERVE is the job's (D, M) or None.  RANKED is a list of {host, fits_now, score,
    load_per_core, free, line_room, ceiling, waiting, load, nproc, note} best first
    (room_of: what fits_now and score go by; note_of: what is to be said about a host
    with no free room to rank by, or none after its line); DROPPED is {host: [reasons]}.
    This is the whole decision, and it touches nothing: tests call it with plain dicts."""
    rows = rows or {}
    res = tuple(reserve) if reserve else (SCORE_FLOOR, SCORE_FLOOR)
    ranked, dropped = [], {}
    for host in sorted(probes):
        p = probes[host] or {}
        why = []
        if not p.get("ok"):
            why.append(p.get("error") or "no answer")
        else:
            row = rows.get(host) or {}
            want_arch = row.get("_want_arch")
            arch = row.get("arch") or "amd64"
            machine = {"x86_64": "amd64", "amd64": "amd64", "aarch64": "arm64", "arm64": "arm64"}.get(
                str(p.get("machine", "")).lower())
            if want_arch and arch != want_arch:
                why.append(f"an {arch} host, and the guests are {want_arch}")
            elif machine and machine != arch:
                why.append(f"says it is {p.get('machine')} ({machine}), and its row is {arch}")
            for n in needs:
                if n not in (p.get("caps") or ()):
                    why.append({"zfs": "no host OpenZFS (no zpool)",
                                "rescuer": "no rescuer.qcow2",
                                "rescuer-uefi": "no rescuer-uefi.qcow2",
                                "debs": "no f2fs-tools and jfsutils debs"}[n])
            for g in guests:
                if g not in (p.get("vms") or ()):
                    why.append(f"no vms/{g} image")
            # cmp-run.sh --ceiling failed: it can run no job here at all (it exits 1
            # with "no tmpfs at /mnt/bko-ram" before it reads the option), so the host
            # is dropped with what it said, and never printed as the one to use.
            crc = p.get("ceiling_rc")
            if crc is not None and crc != 0:
                why.append(p.get("ceiling_err") or f"cmp-run.sh --ceiling failed (exit {crc})")
            elif crc is not None and not p.get("ceiling"):
                why.append("cmp-run.sh --ceiling said no figures")
            if reserve and p.get("ceiling") and not fits(p["ceiling"], reserve):
                c = p["ceiling"]
                why.append(f"--reserve {num(reserve[0])} {num(reserve[1])} is over its "
                           f"ceiling {num(c[0])} {num(c[1])}")
            if p.get("no_cmp_run"):
                why.append(f"no {p['no_cmp_run']}")
        if why:
            dropped[host] = why
            continue
        free = p.get("free")
        room, ahead = room_of(p)
        ranked.append({"host": host, "free": free, "line_room": p.get("line_room"),
                       "ceiling": p.get("ceiling"),
                       "waiting": p.get("waiting"), "load": p.get("load"), "nproc": p.get("nproc"),
                       "fits_now": fits(room, res) and not ahead, "score": round(score_of(room, res), 2),
                       "load_per_core": round(load_per_core(p, rows.get(host) or {}), 2),
                       "note": note_of(p)})
    ranked.sort(key=lambda x: (not x["fits_now"], -x["score"], x["load_per_core"], x["host"]))
    return ranked, dropped


def room_of(p):
    """(ROOM, AHEAD): the room a new --reserve would be offered on the probed host, and
    whether a line it can't be judged against stands before it.

    With --free's "after" line (line_room), ROOM is that: the room left once the waiters
    cmp-run.sh doesn't let a newcomer pass have theirs (one that fits now, or one that
    has waited its BACKFILL_MAX), within the free room.  A --free older than that line
    gives only the free room, and its cmp-run.sh serves the line strictly in order, so a
    job there waits behind anyone in line who could ever be met: AHEAD is then true
    unless the line is known to be empty (waiting 0), and the host never fits now while
    it is.  That is conservative: such a line may hold only waiters that can never start,
    and a job would start there at once."""
    line = p.get("line_room")
    if line is not None:
        free = p.get("free")
        if free is not None:
            line = (min(line[0], free[0]), min(line[1], free[1]))
        return line, False
    return p.get("free"), p.get("waiting") != 0


def note_of(p):
    """A kept host's note, or None: what is to be said about one that answered but has no
    free room to rank by, or no room after its line.  Its ceiling worked (one whose didn't
    is dropped), so this is a cmp-run.sh too old to know --free, or to say what is left
    after its line (sync testvm/ there either way), or a --free that failed, which is
    said in its own words."""
    if p.get("free"):
        if p.get("line_room") is None:
            return ("its cmp-run.sh --free has no \"after\" line (sync testvm/ there): "
                    "it fits now only with nobody in line")
        return None
    if p.get("free_err"):
        return f"its cmp-run.sh --free failed: {p['free_err']}"
    if p.get("free_rc"):
        return f"its cmp-run.sh --free failed (exit {p['free_rc']})"
    return "its cmp-run.sh has no --free (sync testvm/ there)"


def load_per_core(probe, row):
    """A host's load a core, by which equally roomy hosts are ranked: its 1-minute
    loadavg over its cores, and LOAD_PENALTY more load on the shared host (SHARED_ROLES:
    Chopin holds the fleet, the store mirror and the Bigfoot link, every session reaches
    for it first, and its cooler is the weakest), so an idle small host wins a tie."""
    load = probe.get("load")
    cores = probe.get("nproc") or 1
    if load is None:
        return float("inf")
    if set(row.get("roles") or ()) & set(SHARED_ROLES):
        load += LOAD_PENALTY
    return load / max(1, cores)


def split_list(words):
    """--guest a,b --guest c -> ["a", "b", "c"], each once, in order."""
    out = []
    for w in words or ():
        for item in (x.strip() for x in str(w).split(",")):
            if item and item not in out:
                out.append(item)
    return out


def candidates(table, args, guests):
    """The hosts to probe, in the table's order, the architecture they must be of, and the
    hosts dropped before any probe: (hosts, arch, dropped).

    --hosts as given (each an enabled row: table.known() has the enabled ones only, so a
    reserved row is "no such host"), else the default hosts of the guests' architecture.
    The architecture is worked out from --guest either way, and a candidate of the other
    one is dropped here, with a reason, rather than probed: --hosts is a choice among the
    hosts, not a way past the check that a host can run the guests at all (Bigfoot is
    arm64, and an amd64 run has no business asking it anything).  A ValueError says what
    is wrong with --hosts or --guest."""
    arches = {table.guest_arch(g) for g in guests} or {"amd64"}
    if len(arches) > 1:
        raise ValueError("--guest names guests of both architectures: "
                         f"{', '.join(sorted(arches))} (one host can run only one)")
    arch = next(iter(arches))
    named = split_list(args.hosts)
    if named:
        known = table.known()
        bad = [h for h in named if h not in known]
        if bad:
            raise ValueError(f"no such host {', '.join(bad)} (known: {', '.join(known)})")
        hosts = [h for h in table.names() if h in named]
    else:
        hosts = list(table.names(default=True, arch=arch))
    if args.cls:
        hosts = [h for h in hosts if table.host_class(h) == args.cls]
    hosts = [h for h in hosts if h not in split_list(args.exclude)]
    dropped = {}
    for h in list(hosts):          # a no-op without --hosts: those are of ARCH already
        if table.host_arch(h) != arch:
            dropped[h] = [f"an {table.host_arch(h)} host, and the guests are {arch}"]
            hosts.remove(h)
    return hosts, arch, dropped


def parse_args(argv):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="pick-host", add_help=True,
                                description="which KVM test host has room for a job now "
                                            "(testvm/pick-host's header says more)")
    p.add_argument("--reserve", nargs=2, metavar=("D", "M"), type=float)
    p.add_argument("--guest", action="append", default=[], metavar="G")
    p.add_argument("--needs", action="append", default=[], metavar="N")
    p.add_argument("--class", dest="cls", choices=("big", "small"))
    p.add_argument("--hosts", action="append", default=[], metavar="H,H")
    p.add_argument("--exclude", action="append", default=[], metavar="H,H")
    p.add_argument("--count", type=int, metavar="N")
    p.add_argument("--all", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--field", choices=FIELDS, default="ssh")
    p.add_argument("--timeout", type=float, default=20.0)
    a = p.parse_args(argv)
    if a.reserve and (a.reserve[0] < 0 or a.reserve[1] < 0):
        p.error("--reserve D M: GiB, neither under 0")
    if a.count is not None and a.count < 1:
        p.error("--count N: at least 1")
    if a.timeout <= 0:
        p.error("--timeout S: seconds, over 0")
    needs = split_list(a.needs)
    bad = [n for n in needs if n not in NEEDS]
    if bad:
        p.error(f"--needs {', '.join(bad)}: one of {', '.join(NEEDS)}")
    guests = split_list(a.guest)
    bad = [g for g in guests if not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.+-]*", g)]
    if bad:
        p.error(f"--guest {', '.join(bad)}: a guest name (a letter or digit first)")
    return a, guests, needs


def main(argv=None):
    args, guests, needs = parse_args(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else list(argv))

    def as_json(rc, **kw):
        """--json on stdout, whatever the exit status: the keys are always the same ones,
        so a caller may read them without knowing how far pick-host got.  (Only argparse's
        own usage errors, which happen before --json is known, are plain text.)"""
        if args.json:
            out = {"reserve": args.reserve, "guests": guests, "needs": needs, "error": None,
                   "candidates": [], "probes": {}, "ranked": [], "dropped": {}}
            out.update(kw)
            print(json.dumps(out, indent=1, sort_keys=True, default=str))
        return rc

    try:
        table = _host_table()
    except ImportError as e:
        print(e, file=sys.stderr)
        return as_json(2, error=str(e))
    try:
        hosts, arch, dropped_early = candidates(table, args, guests)
    except (ValueError, table.HostsError) as e:
        print(f"pick-host: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return as_json(2, error=str(e))
    if not hosts and not dropped_early:
        what = ["--hosts" if split_list(args.hosts) else f"the default {arch} hosts"]
        if args.cls:
            what.append(f"--class {args.cls}")
        if split_list(args.exclude):
            what.append("--exclude")
        why = f"no candidate host at all ({' with '.join(what)} leave none)"
        print(f"pick-host: {why}", file=sys.stderr)
        return as_json(1, error=why)
    probes = probe_hosts(table, hosts, guests, args.timeout)
    rows = {}
    for h in hosts:
        r = dict(table.row(h))
        r["arch"] = table.host_arch(h)
        r["_want_arch"] = arch
        rows[h] = r
    ranked, dropped = rank(probes, args.reserve, guests, needs, rows)
    dropped.update(dropped_early)        # the wrong-arch hosts, dropped before the probe
    considered = hosts + [h for h in dropped_early if h not in hosts]
    if args.json:
        return as_json(0 if ranked else 1, candidates=considered, probes=probes,
                       ranked=ranked, dropped=dropped)
    if not ranked:
        print("pick-host: no host can take this job now or ever:", file=sys.stderr)
        for h in considered:
            print(f"  {h}: {'; '.join(dropped.get(h) or ['dropped'])}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    want = len(ranked) if args.all else (args.count or 1)
    for x in ranked[:want]:
        print(table.ssh(x["host"]) if args.field == "ssh" else x["host"])
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
